texts=[
# QUESTION LAB
[
('Start with a noticed difference.','This inquiry course practises moving from a visible detail to a question that can be investigated. Use the fictional library scene as a prompt, not as research evidence.','An adult learner notices two differently coloured notebooks. The colour is visible; who uses them and why are unknown. The first task is to keep those statements separate.','Observation → question → assumption → evidence → revision','Record two visible details and one thing the picture cannot tell you.'),
('Build a question tree.','Put one broad curiosity at the root and divide it into smaller questions. Each branch should ask for a different kind of information rather than repeat the same judgement.','Root: how might a study corner support discussion? Branches might concern the purpose of the session, the material people use and how they choose a place to sit. None is established by a picture.','Root question / three branches / one selected leaf','Write a root question, three branches and the narrowest question worth pursuing first.'),
('Make an assumption card.','An assumption is a provisional explanation, not a finding. Give it a short name and record what you expect to observe if it were useful.','Assumption A: a shared sketch might help a discussion stay focused. A possible observation is whether speakers refer to the same sketch. Other explanations, such as a facilitator prompt, must remain visible.','Assumption / expected observation / alternative explanation','Complete one card without using the words proven or always.'),
('Request the evidence you need.','A useful evidence request names the question, the kind of material required and the limit of what that material could support. Do not collect personal data for this exercise.','For the shared-sketch question, an invented transcript with references to objects would be more relevant than a photograph of an attractive room. A transcript still would not establish a causal effect.','Question / source type / relevance / limitation','Write an evidence request that another learner could understand without seeing your notes.'),
('Try a paper experiment.','Use two short invented dialogue extracts to practise applying the same rule. This is a desk exercise in consistent reading, not an experiment involving real participants.','Extract A: I mean the left-hand shape. Extract B: I prefer this version. Code whether each sentence explicitly refers to a shared object. Keep the original wording beside your code.','Extract / coding rule / code / uncertainty','Apply one coding rule to both extracts and flag any ambiguous phrase.'),
('Compare without inventing a winner.','A comparison should show the criterion before the judgement. If the material is too thin, an unresolved result is more useful than a confident ranking.','Sentence A names a shape; sentence B names a preference. That difference supports a description of the sentences, not a conclusion about which group learned more.','Criterion / example A / example B / bounded conclusion','Write a comparison that stays within what the two extracts actually contain.'),
('Invite a challenge.','Peer feedback should identify a claim, ask which material supports it and offer one alternative reading. The aim is to improve the inquiry rather than evaluate a person.','A peer might ask whether left-hand refers to a shared diagram or only the speaker’s page. That question exposes a missing context which the coding rule should acknowledge.','Claim under review / challenge / missing context','Write one generous challenge and the exact passage it addresses.'),
('Show your revision.','Keep the earlier wording visible beside the revision so a reader can see which assumption changed. Revision is a traceable decision rather than a replacement of the record.','Earlier: the shared sketch improved discussion. Revised: one invented sentence explicitly names a shape, while the setting and effect on discussion remain unknown.','Before / reason for change / after','Revise one claim and name the evidence boundary that prompted the change.'),
('Transfer the method.','Apply the inquiry sequence to a different fictional scene. Transfer means retaining the reasoning steps while changing the subject, not copying the previous conclusion.','In a courtyard illustration, a learner notices an empty chair. Start with a question about what context is missing; do not infer low attendance, exclusion or preference from the empty seat.','New detail / new question / source request','Draft a fresh question tree for an object outside the library example.'),
('Keep an inquiry record.','Finish with a compact account of the route you took: noticed detail, selected question, assumption, source request and revised claim. A useful record includes an unresolved question.','This course provides no grade or accredited qualification. Its outcome is a more inspectable piece of reasoning and a clear account of which evidence you still do not have.','Detail / question / assumption / revision / next source','Choose the strongest revision in your record and explain why it is more careful.')
],
# STORY
[
('Give the story a working intention.','This writing workshop develops one short fictional scene through drafts, margin comments and revision. Begin with an effect you want the reader to experience rather than a polished plot.','Working intention: a reader gradually recognises that two friends disagree about a shared memory. This is a creative premise, not an account of real people.','Intended effect / scene boundary / open question','Write a one-sentence intention and one thing the scene will deliberately leave unresolved.'),
('Build a character through choices.','Describe what a character wants in this scene, what they avoid and what they do when those wants conflict. A list of personality adjectives is not yet a dramatic action.','Mara wants to return a notebook but avoids explaining why she kept it. She places it on the far edge of the table. That action gives the reader something to interpret.','Want / avoidance / visible action','Create two actions that reveal different sides of the same fictional character.'),
('Map the scene’s usable space.','Choose a few spatial anchors that matter to the action. The map is an author’s aid, not a measured plan or a claim about an actual room.','A table separates two chairs; a window faces the rain; a door interrupts the conversation. Only mention an object if it affects attention, movement or meaning.','Anchor / relation / narrative use','List three anchors and the moment when each becomes relevant.'),
('Raise a specific tension.','A scene gains tension when a character’s next action becomes harder or more consequential. Escalation need not mean danger; it can be a growing mismatch between what is said and intended.','Mara says she found the notebook yesterday. Her friend notices an older bookmark inside it. The new detail complicates the explanation without requiring the narrator to announce a lie.','Initial situation / complication / choice','Add one complication that changes what the character can comfortably say next.'),
('Draft before polishing.','Write a short first version with a beginning action, a change and a closing beat. Leave sentence-level refinement until the sequence is readable.','An opening might place the notebook on the table. The change could be a question about the bookmark. The closing beat might be a character choosing not to answer.','Opening action / turning beat / ending image','Draft a short scene using those three beats in your own words.'),
('Let dialogue carry a second meaning.','For each line, separate the spoken words from what the speaker hopes to achieve. Subtext should emerge through context rather than a secret motive invented after every sentence.','Line: You kept the blue cover. Surface meaning concerns an object; possible intention is to ask whether a shared memory still matters. Both interpretations require support from the surrounding scene.','Spoken line / intended move / contextual clue','Annotate two lines and remove any explanation already obvious from the dialogue.'),
('Choose who can know what.','Point of view determines which perceptions and thoughts the text can present directly. A change of perspective should be deliberate and easy for a reader to follow.','From Mara’s close perspective, the friend’s silence is observable but the friend’s private intention is not. Replace certainty about that intention with an action or Mara’s own tentative interpretation.','Observer / available knowledge / unavailable thought','Find one sentence that knows too much and rewrite it from the chosen viewpoint.'),
('Make the revision visible.','Compare an earlier passage with a revised one and name the change in effect. Do not describe every edit as improvement; choose a specific intention to test.','Before: She was nervous. After: She turned the notebook until its spine faced her. The revision replaces a label with an action but may leave more room for ambiguity.','Before / after / intended effect / trade-off','Rewrite one labelled emotion as an action and record what the change loses or gains.'),
('Assemble a small portfolio.','Collect the scene intention, first draft, two annotations and revised draft. The portfolio should allow a reader to follow the writing decisions without mistaking fiction for testimony.','Use version labels and a brief change note. Keep any real personal material out of the exercise unless you have independently resolved appropriate permissions.','Artifact / version / purpose / change note','Select four artifacts that best show how the scene developed.'),
('Write an author’s reflection.','Reflect on a decision rather than judging whether you are a good writer. Explain one choice, its effect and a question you would test in another draft.','You might keep the unanswered question at the end because it sustains uncertainty, while noting that another reader may need one clearer clue earlier in the scene.','Choice / effect / uncertainty / next draft','Describe one deliberate trade-off and a concrete next revision.')
],
# VISUAL
[
('Read an image in layers.','This course treats visual reading as a sequence of description, attention and interpretation. The illustrations are authored images, not documentary evidence of real places.','Start with what is present, then ask how it is arranged, then consider possible meanings. Keep those three layers visible in your notes.','Visible elements / arrangement / possible reading','Describe the classroom image in three sentences, one for each layer.'),
('Mark the composition.','Locate the largest shapes and their relationship to the frame. Describe position and proportion in ordinary language rather than claiming exact measurements from a stylised illustration.','A bright table may sit low in the frame while a dark wall forms the background. The contrast can guide attention without proving which object matters to the people shown.','Foreground / middle ground / background / focal area','Sketch a simple composition map using no more than five labelled shapes.'),
('Build a colour vocabulary.','Name hue, relative lightness and contrast separately. A colour description is more precise when it explains a relationship instead of assigning a universal mood.','Cobalt blue beside mustard yellow creates a strong visible contrast. Calling that contrast cheerful is an interpretation that another viewer may not share.','Colour pair / contrast / tentative association','Describe two colour relationships and label any emotional association as your interpretation.'),
('Trace the order of attention.','Record where you looked first, second and third, then ask which visible features might explain that route. Your viewing order is a personal observation, not a population result.','A pale sheet against a dark tabletop may attract attention through contrast. Another reader may notice a face first. Neither route is a measured general rule.','First noticed / next noticed / visible cue','Write your attention sequence and compare it with a second possible route.'),
('Distinguish object from symbol.','An object can support more than one symbolic reading. Connect a proposed meaning to the image and its context rather than treating a symbol as a fixed dictionary entry.','An open notebook might suggest learning, unfinished work or simply a useful tabletop object. The image alone does not decide among those readings.','Object / possible meaning / supporting clue / uncertainty','Offer two readings of one object without selecting a definitive meaning.'),
('Compare two visual choices.','Use the same comparison dimensions for both images. A consistent comparison is easier to inspect than a sequence of personal preferences.','Compare where the brightest area appears, how figures are grouped and which edges are cropped. Avoid turning a compositional difference into a claim about actual behaviour.','Dimension / image A / image B / interpretation','Complete a three-row comparison using composition, contrast and grouping.'),
('Ask where the image came from.','Record authorship, production method and what the source actually supplies. These case illustrations are AI-generated editorial images, with individual provenance listed alongside the document.','A source note establishes how an asset was selected; it does not establish that the depicted lesson took place. Keep illustration provenance separate from claims about a real subject.','Asset / production method / source note / evidence limit','Write a caption that identifies an illustration without presenting it as a photograph of an event.'),
('Name the edge of interpretation.','List what cannot be established from the image. Being explicit about the boundary makes a visual analysis more useful, not less imaginative.','Attendance, accessibility, learning outcomes and relationships between the figures are not supplied by the illustration. These would require relevant independent information.','Interpretation / missing context / needed source','Choose one tempting claim and name the source that would be needed to examine it.'),
('Compose a short visual analysis.','Bring description, arrangement and interpretation into a compact paragraph. Mark the transition between an observable feature and your proposed reading.','A useful sentence might begin The bright table contrasts with the darker wall, followed by I read this as a focus on shared work. The wording keeps the interpretation attributable to the reader.','Description / arrangement / interpretation / boundary','Write a short analysis with one clearly marked interpretive sentence.'),
('Keep a visual reading archive.','Save the source note, composition map, comparison and final analysis together. The archive records your reading process rather than certifying a correct interpretation.','Use an earlier and later caption to show how your language became more precise. Record one question that the images could not answer.','Artifact / revision / lesson / next question','Choose one sentence whose evidence boundary became clearer and explain the revision.')
],
# PROJECT
[
('Define a learning challenge.','This studio course uses a fictional tabletop display project to practise planning, critique and iteration. The result is a learning artifact, not an approved product or installation.','Challenge: communicate three ideas using a small paper model. The audience and setting are invented so learners can focus on how choices connect to an intention.','Challenge / audience assumption / learning goal','Write the challenge in one sentence and name the skill you want to practise.'),
('Agree a learning contract.','A learning contract describes how the exercise will be approached and what kind of feedback will help. It does not assign real employment duties or grant formal certification.','A learner might commit to keeping two alternatives, recording one reason for each choice and asking for feedback about clarity rather than taste.','Practice commitment / feedback request / boundary','Write three commitments that you can actually observe in your own process.'),
('Split the challenge into questions.','Break the broad task into questions about message, form and interpretation. Keep tasks distinct from questions so activity does not become a substitute for learning.','Make three cards: What should a viewer notice first? Which arrangement supports that intention? What would show that the message remains unclear?','Message question / form question / evaluation question','Turn one broad challenge into three answerable design questions.'),
('Map the resources you have.','Separate supplied materials, borrowed references and missing information. This exercise provides illustrations and a document structure, not a real workshop inventory.','A fictional resource list might contain paper, a pencil and an example image. A real making session would need its own material suitability and appropriate setting checks.','Available / reference / missing / needs review','Build a resource map without presenting invented supplies as a real allocation.'),
('Make the work visible.','Use a small task board to distinguish proposed work, work being tried and work ready for discussion. A status is a local learning record, not an official project approval.','Move draft three labels from proposed to trying only after you begin the draft. Keep an unresolved question attached to work that is ready for feedback.','Proposed / trying / discuss / unresolved','Place three learning tasks on the board and explain one status choice.'),
('Keep a process log.','Record a choice while its context is still available. A process log is more informative when it includes a rejected option and the reason for the rejection.','Entry: tried a circular arrangement to suggest equal importance; rejected it because the starting point became hard to find. This is a design judgement, not a measured user result.','Attempt / reason / observed issue / next move','Write two dated-by-version entries rather than inventing a real project calendar.'),
('Ask for a focused critique.','Present the intention, one artifact and one question. Focused critique makes it easier to distinguish a preference from a concern about the stated goal.','Instead of Do you like it? ask Which label did you notice first, and what in the arrangement drew your attention? Use invented peer responses for this document exercise.','Intention / artifact / question / feedback note','Draft a critique request that a peer could answer specifically.'),
('Compare an iteration.','Show what changed, why it changed and which question remains open. A later version is not automatically better; it may exchange one strength for another.','Moving the first label upward may clarify the starting point while reducing symmetry. Record that trade-off rather than claiming the revision solved everything.','Version A / version B / reason / trade-off','Document one change and one unresolved consequence.'),
('Explain the artifact.','A short outcome statement connects the challenge, the selected approach and the limits of the result. Do not claim real audience validation when the course used invented examples.','The statement can describe the intended reading order and point to the layout that supports it. It should also say that no real audience study was supplied.','Challenge / approach / supporting artifact / limit','Write an outcome statement that another learner can inspect against the artifact.'),
('Plan the next learning cycle.','Close by choosing a question that arose from the work rather than adding every possible improvement to an endless list. A focused next cycle is easier to evaluate.','If the starting point remains unclear, the next exercise might compare two label arrangements using a consistent observation prompt. That is a proposed learning task, not a scheduled real study.','Lesson / next question / proposed exercise / evidence need','Choose one next cycle and explain why it follows from this one.')
],
# DEBATE
[
('Set a bounded discussion question.','This course practises building and examining arguments. The issue is fictional: should an imagined reading group use one shared text or offer several choices?','The aim is to make reasoning visible, not to declare a universally correct policy or evaluate a real group. Keep the discussion within the stated exercise.','Issue / scope / decision not being made','Write the discussion question and one real-world conclusion it cannot settle.'),
('Agree on the important terms.','Arguments become difficult to compare when speakers use the same word in different ways. Define the terms needed for this discussion before collecting reasons.','Shared text could mean one extract for one meeting, not a permanent reading rule. Choice could refer to selecting among three extracts rather than unrestricted material.','Term / working meaning / excluded meaning','Define shared, choice and useful for this particular exercise.'),
('Map a claim to its reasons.','State a claim, the reason offered for it and the assumption connecting them. A reason is not yet evidence, and the connecting assumption may be disputed.','Claim: one extract could focus the conversation. Reason: everyone can refer to the same passage. Assumption: a common reference is helpful for the intended discussion.','Claim / reason / connecting assumption','Build a three-part argument without claiming an observed learning outcome.'),
('Support the first position.','Develop the strongest bounded version of the shared-text argument. Separate a plausible mechanism from information that would be needed to establish it in practice.','A common passage may make references easier to locate. Whether that actually helps a particular group remains a question requiring suitable observation and participant context.','Supporting reason / mechanism / evidence needed','Write one strong supporting reason and the source needed to test its relevance.'),
('Represent the alternative fairly.','Build a version of the multiple-choice argument that its supporter would recognise. Do not weaken it simply to make your preferred position easier to defend.','Choice might let readers connect discussion to their interests. It could also make references harder to share. Both points belong in an honest comparison.','Alternative claim / strongest reason / acknowledged cost','Write the alternative argument before adding your response.'),
('Evaluate a source before using it.','Ask whether a source addresses the actual question, how it was produced and what it cannot establish. This exercise supplies no real empirical study.','A fictional preference quote can illustrate a viewpoint but cannot establish what most readers prefer. A beautiful illustration is not evidence about reading outcomes.','Source type / relevance / limit / permitted use','Classify an invented quotation and explain the narrow use you would allow.'),
('Ask a question that could matter.','A useful challenge targets an assumption or a missing link rather than the speaker’s character. Give the other person room to clarify the argument.','Question: does the benefit of a common reference depend on the kind of discussion? That asks for a condition rather than assuming that the original claim is foolish.','Target claim / questioned assumption / clarification request','Write two respectful challenges addressed to different parts of the argument.'),
('Respond without changing the subject.','A response should identify the challenge, answer the part you can address and acknowledge what remains unresolved. It is acceptable to narrow the original claim.','A revised response might say a shared extract could help a close-reading exercise, while a broader discussion might benefit from several choices. The claim is now more conditional.','Challenge / response / qualification / unresolved issue','Answer one question and add a qualification that genuinely changes the claim.'),
('Revise the argument map.','Update the claim, reasons and assumptions together. If the claim becomes narrower, check that the supporting reasons still address the revised version.','Replace one text is best with one shared extract may suit a session focused on close comparison. The revision concerns task fit rather than a universal ranking.','Earlier claim / revised claim / retained reason / removed assumption','Revise the map and cross out a reason that no longer fits.'),
('State a provisional position.','Finish with the position you can currently support, its strongest reason and a condition that could change your view. A provisional position is not a failure to decide.','For this invented close-reading session, a shared extract is a defensible starting option. Information about the actual group, purpose and materials could change that choice.','Position / reason / condition / open question','Write a closing position that leaves its evidence boundary visible.')
],
# SELF PACED
[
('Choose a useful starting point.','This self-paced course helps an adult learner practise reading and summarising a short fictional passage. Start by identifying which part of that task feels least clear.','You may want practice locating a main idea, distinguishing an example from a claim or writing a concise summary. This self-check is a personal choice, not a diagnostic assessment.','Current comfort / difficult step / chosen focus','Select one focus and describe it in observable terms.'),
('Set a small learning goal.','A useful goal describes an action you can review in your own work. Avoid a broad promise such as become an excellent reader by the end of the document.','Example: write a two-sentence summary that keeps the main claim and one qualification. The goal concerns a written artifact rather than a certificate or score.','Action / artifact / review question','Write a goal you can check against a short piece of your own writing.'),
('Pick a route through the practice.','Choose a route based on your focus while retaining the same final reflection. Route A starts with the main claim; route B starts with examples; route C starts with uncertainty.','Suggested route A: pages 4, 5, 6, 9, 10. Route B: pages 5, 4, 7, 9, 10. Route C: pages 6, 7, 8, 9, 10. You can revisit any page.','Route A / route B / route C / return point','Choose a route and name the reason it fits your present question.'),
('Take a note that separates roles.','Label the main claim, supporting example and qualification in an invented passage. Notes are easier to reuse when they preserve these distinctions.','Passage: A shared sketch may focus a conversation; for example, a speaker can point to a shape. This depends on the discussion task. The final sentence qualifies the first.','Claim / example / qualification','Make a three-part note without turning may into will.'),
('Try a summary station.','Write a short summary of the passage using fewer words while preserving its uncertainty. Concision should not erase the condition that makes the claim accurate.','Possible draft: A shared sketch can provide a common reference, but its usefulness depends on the task. This is a summary of an invented passage, not a research finding.','Original idea / shorter wording / retained condition','Write your own two-sentence summary and underline its qualification.'),
('Review with a feedback key.','Compare the meaning of your summary with the source passage. The key checks whether a claim, example or condition disappeared; it does not assign a grade.','If your draft says shared sketches improve all discussions, it has expanded the original claim. If it merely lists a shape, it may have retained the example but lost the main idea.','Claim retained / example handled / condition retained','Identify one expansion or omission in your draft and explain its effect.'),
('Use a targeted support page.','Return to the step that caused difficulty rather than restarting the whole course. A support exercise should address a specific confusion.','If examples dominate your notes, highlight only the sentence that makes the general claim. If uncertainty disappears, copy the conditional phrase before writing a new summary.','Difficulty / support move / new attempt','Choose one support move and use it on the same passage.'),
('Apply the method to a new passage.','Transfer the reading routine to another invented statement. Keep the main claim and its boundary visible instead of borrowing the conclusion from the earlier example.','New passage: offering several texts might invite different interests; however, a discussion needs a way to share references. Identify the claim and the condition before summarising.','New claim / condition / concise version','Write a fresh summary and note one difference from the shared-sketch passage.'),
('Collect the learning artifacts.','Bring your first note, first summary, feedback note and revised summary together. A small portfolio makes the route visible even when you took the pages out of order.','Label each artifact by version, not an invented completion date. Include one line explaining why the revised version better matches the source.','Artifact / version / change / reason','Select four artifacts and arrange them so another reader can follow the revision.'),
('Choose the next route.','Reflect on the step that became clearer and the difficulty that remains. The next route should follow from that observation rather than a generic instruction to practise more.','You might continue with conditional claims, compare two passages or practise separating examples from explanations. No result about real learner performance is supplied by this template.','Clearer step / remaining difficulty / next practice','Name one next practice task and the artifact you will use to review it.')
]
]
styles=[('inquiry','#25375f','#e4e9f7'),('manuscript','#8d3655','#f7e5eb'),('visual','#244fa2','#e5edfb'),('studio','#176b6a','#e0f2ef'),('argument','#67413b','#f2e7df'),('pathway','#65448d','#ece5f6')]
sources=[['tideline/04','swatch/03','common-ground/02','afterglow/11'],['afterglow/05','afterglow/11','common-ground/04','open-shelf/06'],['swatch/02','tideline/02','open-shelf/09','swatch/04'],['swatch/01','open-shelf/03','tideline/09','swatch/05'],['afterglow/09','common-ground/03','open-shelf/06','tideline/15'],['tideline/16','afterglow/11','common-ground/05','swatch/04']]
names=['QUESTION LAB','STORY WORKSHOP','VISUAL LITERACY','PROJECT STUDIO','DEBATE ROOM','SELF-PACED ROUTE']
specs=[]
for idx,(course,chapters,style) in enumerate(zip(courses,texts,styles)):
 id=course['id'];layout,color,wash=style;directory=r/'public/atelier'/id;(directory/'images').mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
 work=dict(id=id,title=names[idx],subtitle=course['title'],category='课程',format='文档',status='published',pages=10,illustrations=5,expectedIllustrations=5,framework=course['framework'],scope='Fictional adult learning exercises. Illustrations are not records of real participants or research evidence. No accredited qualification or actual learner outcomes are supplied.',layout=layout,color=color,wash=wash,expansion='documents-50',newIllustrations=1,curatedIllustrations=4)
 pages=[]
 for j,(title,lead,body,subheading,question) in enumerate(chapters):
  pages.append(dict(section=f'{j+1:02d} / {names[idx]}',title=title,lead=lead,body=body,subheading=subheading,detail='Working record: '+question+' Keep your first attempt and your revised version so the change remains visible.',question=question,image=j//2+1,kind='cover' if j==0 else 'appendix' if j==9 else 'body',module=course['sectionOutline'][j]))
 provenance=[dict(image=1,source='New AI-generated illustration for '+id),*[dict(image=n+2,source='Curated existing AI-generated illustration: '+src+'.jpg')for n,src in enumerate(sources[idx])]]
 for n,src in enumerate([pathlib.Path('.cache/expansion-50')/(id+'.jpg')]+[r/'public/atelier'/(s+'.jpg') for s in sources[idx]]):
  # Source assets are numbered within images/ in the earlier cases.
  if n:case,num=sources[idx][n-1].split('/');src=r/'public/atelier'/case/'images'/(num+'.jpg')
  if n==0 and not src.exists():src=directory/'images'/'01.jpg'
  im=Image.open(src).convert('RGB');im.thumbnail((1100,800));im.save(directory/'images'/f'{n+1:02d}.jpg',quality=84,optimize=True)
 data=dict(work=work,pages=pages,provenance=provenance);(directory/'data.json').write_text(json.dumps(data,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n');(directory/'content.js').write_text('export const work='+json.dumps(work,ensure_ascii=False)+';\nexport const imageCount=5;\nexport const pages='+json.dumps(pages,ensure_ascii=False)+';\n')
 specs.append(dict(id=id,title=names[idx],category='课程',layout=layout))
(r/'scripts/course-expansion-content.json').write_text(json.dumps(specs,indent=2)+'\n')
print('Authored six distinct 10-page courses / 60 substantive sections / 6 new heroes + 24 curated images')
